{# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Stand-size and floor-plan visuals.

   Every one of these is inline SVG rendered on the server. There is no chart library and
   there is no <script>: the Content-Security-Policy on this site carries no 'unsafe-inline'
   and no CDN host, so a chart that needs either is a chart that silently does not draw. What
   is left — geometry the server already computed, in markup — turns out to be exactly right
   for these four, because all four are showing SIZE and none of them needs to animate.

   ── HOW IDENTITY IS CARRIED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Never by colour alone. Every block, every segment and every swatch carries a text label or
   a legend entry beside it, and the same numbers appear in the table on the page. Three of
   the eight categorical hues sit below 3:1 against this white surface — legible as a filled
   shape, not as a lone signal — so the labels are the mechanism, not decoration.

   Hover is the SVG <title> element, which every browser surfaces as a native tooltip and
   every screen reader reads as the accessible name. It costs nothing and needs no script.
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}

{# The categorical order is fixed and assigned by slot, never cycled by rank — a colour that
   moves when a stand type is added beside it is a colour that means nothing. Validated
   against this screen's white surface: every adjacent pair clears the colour-vision and
   normal-vision separation floors. Three slots fall below 3:1 contrast, which is why every
   mark on this page is directly labelled. #}
{% macro colour(i) %}{{ ['#2a78d6','#eb6834','#1baf7a','#eda100','#e87ba4','#008300','#4a3aa7','#e34948'][i % 8] }}{% endmacro %}


{# ── 1 · TO-SCALE SIZE SWATCH ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   One stand type drawn at a shared scale, with a human figure for reference.

   The figure is the whole point. "3 × 3 m" is a number an organiser reads and does not feel;
   a rectangle with a person in it is a size they can judge against the gazebo they hired. All
   the swatches on the page share one scale, so a 6 × 3 is visibly twice a 3 × 3 — which is
   the comparison that stops a plan going wrong. #}
{% macro swatch(type, px_per_m, label, size) %}
  {% import _self as viz %}
  {% set w = (type.width_cm / 100) * px_per_m %}
  {% set d = (type.depth_cm / 100) * px_per_m %}
  {% set wm = (type.width_cm / 100) %}
  {% set dm = (type.depth_cm / 100) %}
  {# The DIMENSION is passed in, because it belongs to the unit the pitch was sold in — a
     6ft stand is not "1.83 × 1.83 m" to the vendor who applied for it, and this string is
     the swatch's only text for a screen reader. The AREA stays m² whatever the unit: it is
     the number the hall is measured against, and nobody hires a hall in square feet here.
     Falls back to metres so a caller with no size string still renders correctly. #}
  {% set size = size|default(wm ~ ' × ' ~ dm ~ ' m') %}
  <svg class="viz-sw" width="{{ (w + 2)|round }}" height="{{ (d + 2)|round }}"
       viewBox="0 0 {{ (w + 2)|round }} {{ (d + 2)|round }}" role="img"
       aria-label="{{ label }}: {{ size }}, {{ (wm * dm)|round(1) }} square metres">
    <title>{{ label }} — {{ size }} ({{ (wm * dm)|round(1) }} m²)</title>
    {# 4px corner radius on the data mark, 2px inset so neighbouring swatches keep a surface gap #}
    <rect x="1" y="1" width="{{ w|round }}" height="{{ d|round }}" rx="4"
          fill="#eef4fc" stroke="#2a78d6" stroke-width="2"></rect>
    {% if w > 30 and d > 34 %}
      {# A 0.5 m-wide person, drawn to the same scale. Not decoration — the reference. #}
      {% set ph = px_per_m * 1.7 %}
      {% if ph < d - 8 %}
        <g transform="translate({{ (w / 2 + 1)|round }},{{ (d - ph / 2 - 3)|round }})" fill="#52514e" opacity=".55">
          <circle cx="0" cy="{{ (-ph * 0.36)|round(1) }}" r="{{ (px_per_m * 0.11)|round(1) }}"></circle>
          <rect x="{{ (-px_per_m * 0.13)|round(1) }}" y="{{ (-ph * 0.27)|round(1) }}"
                width="{{ (px_per_m * 0.26)|round(1) }}" height="{{ (ph * 0.52)|round(1) }}" rx="2"></rect>
        </g>
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
  </svg>
{% endmacro %}


{# ── 2 · FLOOR-AREA BUDGET ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One bar: how much of the sellable floor the published quotas have already spent.

   A single stacked bar rather than a gauge or a donut, because the question is a part-to-
   whole comparison against a hard limit and a bar is the only form that shows the overshoot
   honestly. When it overshoots, the overspill is drawn PAST the 100% line rather than by
   rescaling the bar — rescaling would make an impossible plan look merely full. #}
{% macro budget(plan) %}
  {% set W = 720 %}{% set H = 30 %}
  {% set over = not plan.fits %}
  {% set scale = over ? (plan.usable_sqm + plan.over_sqm) : plan.usable_sqm %}
  {% set usedW = scale > 0 ? (plan.committed_sqm / scale * W) : 0 %}
  {% set limitX = scale > 0 ? (plan.usable_sqm / scale * W) : W %}

  <svg class="viz-bar" viewBox="0 0 {{ W }} {{ H + 22 }}" width="100%" height="{{ H + 22 }}"
       preserveAspectRatio="none" role="img"
       aria-label="{{ plan.committed_sqm }} square metres of stands against {{ plan.usable_sqm }} square metres of sellable floor, {{ plan.used_pct }} per cent{% if over %}. Over by {{ plan.over_sqm }} square metres{% endif %}">
    <title>{{ plan.committed_sqm }} m² committed of {{ plan.usable_sqm }} m² sellable — {{ plan.used_pct }}%</title>

    <rect x="0" y="0" width="{{ W }}" height="{{ H }}" rx="4" fill="#f0efec"></rect>
    {# The committed block, clipped at the limit, then the overspill in the critical status
       colour — which ships with the word "over" beside it, never as colour alone. #}
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="{{ min(usedW, limitX)|round }}" height="{{ H }}" rx="4" fill="#2a78d6"></rect>
    {% if over %}
      <rect x="{{ (limitX + 2)|round }}" y="0" width="{{ (usedW - limitX - 2)|round }}" height="{{ H }}"
            rx="4" fill="#d03b3b"></rect>
    {% endif %}

    {# The sellable-floor limit, drawn as a rule so "full" has a visible edge. #}
    <line x1="{{ limitX|round }}" y1="-2" x2="{{ limitX|round }}" y2="{{ H + 4 }}"
          stroke="#0b0b0b" stroke-width="2"></line>
    <text x="{{ min(limitX + 6, W - 4)|round }}" y="{{ H + 18 }}" font-size="11" fill="#52514e"
          text-anchor="{{ limitX > W - 120 ? 'end' : 'start' }}">
      {{ plan.usable_sqm }} m² sellable</text>
    <text x="2" y="{{ H + 18 }}" font-size="11" fill="#52514e">
      {{ plan.committed_sqm }} m² committed</text>
  </svg>
{% endmacro %}


{# ── 3 · CATEGORY MIX ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One stacked bar of pitches by category, because the quota system exists to control the mix
   and a table of seven numbers does not show a mix. Adjacent segments only, which is the
   pairlist the palette order is validated against; a 2px surface gap between them; and each
   segment wide enough to hold its own count is directly labelled. #}
{% macro mix(plan) %}
  {% import _self as viz %}
  {% if plan.categories is not empty and plan.pitches > 0 %}
  {% set W = 720 %}{% set H = 26 %}
  <svg class="viz-bar" viewBox="0 0 {{ W }} {{ H }}" width="100%" height="{{ H }}"
       preserveAspectRatio="none" role="img"
       aria-label="Pitches by category: {% for c in plan.categories %}{{ c.label }} {{ c.quota }}{{ loop.last ? '' : ', ' }}{% endfor %}">
    <title>{{ plan.pitches }} pitches across {{ plan.categories|length }} categories</title>
    {% set x = 0 %}
    {% for c in plan.categories %}
      {% set w = c.quota / plan.pitches * W %}
      <g>
        <title>{{ c.label }} — {{ c.quota }} pitch(es), {{ c.pct }}% · {{ c.sqm }} m²</title>
        <rect x="{{ x|round }}" y="0" width="{{ max(w - 2, 1)|round }}" height="{{ H }}" rx="4"
              fill="{{ viz.colour(c.index) }}"></rect>
        {% if w > 42 %}
          <text x="{{ (x + w / 2 - 1)|round }}" y="{{ H / 2 + 4 }}" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700"
                text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff">{{ c.quota }}</text>
        {% endif %}
      </g>
      {% set x = x + w %}
    {% endfor %}
  </svg>
  {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}


{# ── 4 · THE BLOCK LAYOUT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The hall as a rectangle with the pitches packed into it, to scale.

   This is the one that has to be honest about what it is not. It packs equal rows into a
   plain rectangle and knows nothing about fire exits, columns, power drops or the loading
   door — so the caller prints that sentence next to it, every time. A diagram that LOOKS like
   a site plan while knowing nothing about fire is worse than no diagram, because somebody
   will forward it to the venue. #}
{% macro floorplan(plan, px_per_m) %}
  {% import _self as viz %}
  {% if plan.measured %}
  {% set W = (plan.floor_w_cm / 100) * px_per_m %}
  {% set D = (plan.floor_d_cm / 100) * px_per_m %}
  <svg class="viz-plan" viewBox="-4 -4 {{ (W + 8)|round }} {{ (D + 8)|round }}"
       width="100%" height="auto" role="img"
       aria-label="Indicative block layout: {{ plan.placed }} of {{ plan.pitches }} pitches placed in a hall {{ (plan.floor_w_cm / 100) }} by {{ (plan.floor_d_cm / 100) }} metres. Not a site plan.">
    <title>{{ (plan.floor_w_cm / 100) }} × {{ (plan.floor_d_cm / 100) }} m hall — {{ plan.placed }} of {{ plan.pitches }} pitches placed</title>

    <rect x="0" y="0" width="{{ W|round }}" height="{{ D|round }}"
          fill="#fcfcfb" stroke="#c3c2b7" stroke-width="2"></rect>

    {% for b in plan.blocks %}
      {% set bx = (b.x / 100) * px_per_m %}
      {% set by = (b.y / 100) * px_per_m %}
      {% set bw = (b.w / 100) * px_per_m %}
      {% set bd = (b.d / 100) * px_per_m %}
      <g>
        <title>{{ b.name }} — {{ b.label }} m</title>
        {# 2px surface ring so neighbouring pitches never merge into one shape. #}
        <rect x="{{ bx|round }}" y="{{ by|round }}" width="{{ bw|round }}" height="{{ bd|round }}"
              rx="3" fill="{{ viz.colour(b.cat_index) }}" fill-opacity=".9"
              stroke="#fcfcfb" stroke-width="2"></rect>
        {# Measured against the ACTUAL string, not a fixed pixel floor. "1.8×0.75" needs three
           times the room "3×3" does, and a width test that ignores that renders ".8×0.75"
           spilling over its neighbour — which is worse than no label, because it reads as a
           different number. Below the threshold the legend and the hover title carry it. #}
        {% if bw > (b.label|length * 6.2) + 6 and bd > 15 %}
          <text x="{{ (bx + bw / 2)|round }}" y="{{ (by + bd / 2 + 4)|round }}" font-size="10.5"
                font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff">{{ b.label }}</text>
        {% endif %}
      </g>
    {% endfor %}

    {# Dimension rules on two sides, so the rectangle is a measurement rather than a shape. #}
    <text x="{{ (W / 2)|round }}" y="-8" font-size="11" fill="#52514e" text-anchor="middle">
      {{ (plan.floor_w_cm / 100) }} m</text>
    <text x="-8" y="{{ (D / 2)|round }}" font-size="11" fill="#52514e" text-anchor="middle"
          transform="rotate(-90 -8 {{ (D / 2)|round }})">{{ (plan.floor_d_cm / 100) }} m</text>
  </svg>
  {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}


{# ── LEGEND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Present whenever two or more series are on screen, because identity must never be colour
   alone — and the swatch beside the name is what lets the colour mean anything at all. #}
{% macro legend(items) %}
  {% import _self as viz %}
  <ul class="viz-legend">
    {% for it in items %}
      <li><span class="viz-legend__dot" style="background:{{ viz.colour(it.index) }}"></span>{{ it.label }}{% if it.suffix is defined %} <em>{{ it.suffix }}</em>{% endif %}</li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
{% endmacro %}
